    bottomLine: "The hack squat to buy if you want the squat, not the leg press, and want the shortest, lightest sled on this page.",
    description: "Titan's dedicated hack squat runs a plate-loaded carriage on linear bearings and steel guide rods at 35 degrees, with flip-in and flip-out racking handles, an adjustable backrest, high-density padding, dual 9.75-inch Olympic weight sleeves and a large diamond-plated footplate that also suits standing calf raises. Titan listed it at $974.99 on 19 October; an earlier Titan listing gave $939.99. We found no hands-on review, so the figures are Titan's: a 700 lb carriage capacity, a 46.75 by 60.5 inch footprint, 45.5 inches tall, a 25.25 by 23.25 inch footplate, a 20 by 11.5 inch back pad and a 190 lb bolt-together frame. An older Titan listing for the same machine gives the same 35-degree angle and 700 lb rating. Titan positions it as the single-station alternative to its combo, which has the higher 1,000 lb capacity. Titan's product page carries only a general disclaimer, so check its warranty terms. We could not confirm an Amazon listing for this model; Titan sells it direct.",
  },
  {
    id: "titan-leg-press-hack-squat",
    name: "Titan Fitness Leg Press Hack Squat Machine",
    brand: "Titan Fitness",
    category: "45-degree plate-loaded hack squat and leg press combo",
    badge: "Best Combo",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Titan+Fitness+Leg+Press+Hack+Squat+Machine+Plate-Loaded&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: null,
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$1,530–$2,550",
    priceBounds: {"low":1530,"high":2550},
    specs: "45° carriage · 84 x 40 x 53 in. (L x W x H) · 325 lb · three lockout positions · 26 x 22 in. hack squat footplate",
    pros: [
      "Hack squat and leg press for one footprint",
      "Titan, a hands-on review and an Amazon listing agree on size, weight and angle",
      "Sturdy and stable in use, per Garage Gym Reviews",
      "Shoulder pad spread and pad sizes published",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Hack squat felt heavier than most commercial machines to one tester",
      "May not suit lifters 6 ft 3 in or taller, per Garage Gym Reviews",
      "Capacity given as 1,000 lb by Titan but 875 lb in an Amazon spec table",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The best-documented machine that does both lifts, for lifters of average height who want a combo.",
    description: "Titan's combo switches between hack squat and leg press by folding the leg press footplate down to reveal the hack squat back pad and lifting the leg press back pad off its hinge, according to Garage Gym Reviews, with three lockout positions on the side handles. Titan listed it on 19 October at $1,529.97, down from $2,549.99. Titan, Garage Gym Reviews and an indexed Amazon listing agree on the 45-degree carriage, the 84 by 40 by 53 inch frame and the 325 lb weight; Titan and the reviewer agree on the 26 by 22 inch hack squat footplate. Titan gives a 7.5 inch shoulder-pad spread, 4.5 by 8 inch shoulder pads, a 20 by 15 inch hack squat back pad, four footplate positions and an 80 lb carriage. Titan and the reviewer give a 1,000 lb capacity, but the Amazon listing's specification table gives 875 lb, so we have left capacity off. Garage Gym Reviews found the hack squat heavier than most commercial machines, thought it would not suit lifters 6 ft 3 in or taller, and reported a one-year warranty; check Titan's current terms.",
  },
  {
    id: "force-usa-compact-hack-squat",
    name: "Force USA Compact Leg Press & Hack Squat (2027 Model)",
    brand: "Force USA",
    category: "Compact 30-degree hack squat, leg press and calf raise",
    badge: "Best for Small Spaces",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=leg+press+hack+squat+machine&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Shop Similar on Amazon",
    image: null,
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $999",
    priceBounds: {"low":999,"high":999},
    specs: "30° incline · 38 lb starting sled · 700 lb of plates plus a 320 lb user · 52 x 65 in. footprint (maker)",
    pros: [
      "Hack squat pad adjusts horizontally and vertically, per Force USA",
      "Lightest starting weight here, at 38 lb",
      "Eight lockout positions and built-in bottom stops",
      "Stated 5 ft 0 in to 6 ft 8 in user range",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The shallow angle needs more plates for the same effort",
      "Earlier generations had different ratings; check it is the 2027 model",
      "Force USA’s page gives two footplate sizes",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The hack squat for a short room, a beginner or a lifter who wants a gentler angle and a light start.",
    description: "Force USA's Compact is a 3-in-1 hack squat, leg press and calf raise on a 30-degree incline, and the updated 2027 model was listed at $999 on 19 October. Force USA and a UK retailer's listing agree on the 30-degree angle, the 38 lb starting sled and the 700 lb plate rating; Force USA says that rating excludes body weight and the machine takes a user of up to 320 lb on top. For the hack squat, Force USA says the pad adjusts independently on horizontal and vertical axes and that shoulder-pad positioning covers users from 5 ft 0 in to 6 ft 8 in. It gives eight lockout positions with safety stops in the sled's travel path, a 52 by 65 inch footprint, a 57 inch height and about 90 minutes to assemble, and says its manual recommends 3 to 5 feet of clearance. Its page gives the footplate as 35 by 29.5 inches in one place and 30 by 29.5 in another, so we have left that off. We could not confirm an Amazon listing; Force USA sells it direct.",
  },
  {
    id: "body-solid-glph1100b-hack",
    name: "Body-Solid GLPH1100B Leg Press & Hack Squat",
    brand: "Body-Solid",
    category: "Plate-loaded hack squat and leg press combo, light commercial",
    badge: "Best Pads",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Body-Solid+GLPH1100+Leg+Press+Hack+Squat&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: null,
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$1,735–$2,200",
    priceBounds: {"low":1735,"high":2200},
    specs: "1,000 lb capacity · 96 x 34 x 56 in. (L x W x H) · 317 lb · three lockout positions · 4 in. contoured shoulder pads",
    pros: [
      "4 in. thick, contoured back and shoulder pads, per Body-Solid",
      "Narrowest combo frame here, at 34 in.",
      "Rated for home and commercial use",
      "Sold by Body-Solid, dealers and on Amazon",
    ],
    cons: [
      "At 96 in. long, it needs a long wall",
      "Carriage weight differs between Body-Solid (60 lb) and Iron Company (80 lb)",
      "Body-Solid does not publish a height range or shoulder-pad spread",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The combo for lifters who value pad comfort and a narrow frame and have the length to spare.",
    description: "The GLPH1100B is a plate-loaded hack squat and leg press on a quad-track roller system, with flip-and-lock back pads, diamond-plate footplates for both positions, under-carriage plate loading, three lockout positions and a 2 by 4 inch 11-gauge steel mainframe. For hack squats, Body-Solid gives the back and shoulder pads as 4 inches thick, double-stitched and contoured, the most detailed pad description of the combos here. Body-Solid listed it in stock at $2,200 on 19 October and Iron Company at $1,735, and an Amazon listing was indexed. Body-Solid, Iron Company and the Amazon listing agree on the 1,000 lb capacity and three lockouts, and Body-Solid and Iron Company on the 96 by 34 inch footprint; Body-Solid gives a 56 inch height and 317 lb weight. Body-Solid gives the empty carriage as 60 lb and Iron Company as 80 lb, so we have left it off. It ships in five boxes. Body-Solid lists both in-home and commercial warranties for it; check which applies to you.",
  },
  {
    id: "bells-of-steel-pandemonium",
    name: "Bells of Steel Pandemonium Squat",
    brand: "Bells of Steel",
    category: "Pendulum squat with calf press and Viking press",
    badge: "Best Pendulum",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=pendulum+squat+machine&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Shop Similar on Amazon",
    image: null,
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $1,750",
    priceBounds: {"low":1750,"high":1750},
    specs: "Maker figures: counterbalanced pendulum arm · 450 lb max capacity · 88 x 48.5 in. footprint · 88 in. tall extended · 380 lb",
    pros: [
      "A pendulum squat, rare in home ranges, with a counterbalance arm",
      "Also does calf raises, Viking presses, dips and pull-ups, per Bells of Steel",
      "Three peg levels for lifters up to about 6 ft 5 in",
      "Removable back pad and spotter arm included",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No independent review yet; figures are Bells of Steel’s",
      "Bells of Steel’s own page gives two different moving-parts warranties",
      "Tall, at about 88 in. extended",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The machine for lifters who specifically want a pendulum squat at home and accept relying on the maker’s figures.",
    description: "The Pandemonium is a pendulum squat: the back pad, shoulder pads and footplate sit on a lever that swings in an arc, with a counterbalance arm that offsets the lever's weight, a single tall upright with three peg levels, and set-ups for calf raises, Viking presses, dips, pull-ups and rows. Bells of Steel listed it at $1,749.99 on 19 October, and an industry newsletter reported its release, but we found no hands-on review. The figures are Bells of Steel's: a 450 lb maximum capacity, 380 lb machine weight, about 88 inches deep with the arm and 88 inches tall fully extended, 48.5 inches wide, a 21.6 inch square footplate, 11.3 inch loadable sleeves and two storage horns for extra 45 lb plates. Bells of Steel says it suits lifters up to about 6 ft 5 in and takes 2.3 inch rack attachments. Its specification table gives a lifetime frame, three-year moving parts and one-year pad warranty, while its warranty section gives 90 days on pins, handles and moving parts; ask before buying. We could not find it on Amazon.",
  },
  {
    id: "steelflex-plhp",
    name: "Steelflex PLHP Plate Loaded Hack Press",
    brand: "Steelflex",
    category: "Commercial-style plate-loaded hack press (lever)",
    badge: "Most Commercial",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=hack+squat+machine&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Shop Similar on Amazon",
    image: null,
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $3,380",
    priceBounds: {"low":3380,"high":3380},
    specs: "Maker figures: plate-loaded lever · 99 x 59 x 52 in. · about 377 lb (171 kg) · 265 lb (120 kg) max user · 15-yr frame warranty",
    pros: [
      "Built and warranted for commercial as well as home use",
      "15-year frame warranty on Steelflex’s data sheet",
      "Sealed bearings at all pivot points and continuous welds",
      "Adjustable for different heights and sizes, per Steelflex",
    ],
    cons: [
      "265 lb maximum user weight, a real limit for a squat machine",
      "The most expensive and largest machine here",
      "No independent review; we could not confirm an Amazon listing",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The hack squat for a well-funded garage gym that wants commercial build and warranty, for lifters inside its user limit.",
    description: "The PLHP is a commercial-style plate-loaded hack press: a lever machine with sealed bearings at all pivots, Olympic weight posts, 50 mm high-density upholstery, continuous welds and adjustment for different heights and sizes, according to Steelflex's data sheet. Select Fitness listed it at $3,380 with free shipping on 19 October; we found no independent review. The figures are Steelflex's: 251 by 149 by 133.2 cm (about 99 by 59 by 52 inches), 171 kg (about 377 lb) and a 120 kg (about 265 lb) maximum user weight. Because your body weight is part of the load in a hack squat, that user limit matters more than on a leg press. Steelflex gives a 12-gauge steel frame in 5 by 10 cm oval tubing, and a warranty of 15 years on the frame, two years on bearings, one year on other parts and six months on upholstery and grips, for commercial and residential use. We could not confirm an Amazon listing; it is sold through fitness dealers.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Home Gym", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/home-gym/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Hack Squat Machines", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-hack-squat-machines/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best hack squat machine for a home gym?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For a dedicated hack squat, Titan's Plate-Loaded Linear Hack Squat, a 35-degree sled on linear bearings for about $975, though its figures are Titan's own. For a machine that also leg presses, Titan's Leg Press Hack Squat combo, whose size, weight and angle three sources agree on. For a short room, the Force USA Compact at 30 degrees.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a hack squat or leg press better for a home gym?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It depends on what you want to train. A hack squat loads you through the shoulders and moves your body with the sled, so it feels closer to a squat and is harder with the same plates. A leg press keeps your body still and loads only through the feet. A combo does both in one footprint, which is why most home buyers choose one.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What angle should a hack squat machine be?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "There is no single right angle. Most combos run at about 45 degrees; Titan's dedicated sled is 35 and Force USA's Compact 30. A steeper sled puts more of your body weight and the plates into the lift; a shallower one leans you back and feels lighter. Compare numbers only on the same machine.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why does a hack squat feel heavier than a leg press?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On a hack squat your body rides the sled, so you lift a share of your own weight as well as the plates: about 71% of it on a 45-degree sled, ignoring friction. On a leg press your body stays still. Garage Gym Reviews also found the hack squat on Titan's combo heavier than on most commercial machines.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much space does a home hack squat machine need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Titan lists its dedicated hack squat at 46.75 by 60.5 inches, the Force USA Compact is 52 by 65, and the leg press combos are 84 to 96 inches long. Allow about three feet around the frame to step in, load plates and swap pads. The Pandemonium is about 88 inches tall, so check the ceiling for that one.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a hack squat machine safe to use alone?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is one of the safer ways to squat hard alone, provided you set the bottom stop just below your deepest safe position every session, practise the lockout levers with an empty sled, load plates evenly and stay within the user weight limit. Talk to a qualified professional first if you have a knee, back, neck or heart condition or high blood pressure.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-10-19",
      dateModified: "2026-10-19",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      ...(product.brand ? { brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.brand } } : {}),
      ...(product.image ? { image: product.image.url } : {}),
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 6,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-hack-squat-machines/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/best-hack-squat-machines/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "home-gym");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const MORE = (GROUP?.pages ?? []).filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-19",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-19",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best hack squat machine",
      "home hack squat machine",
      "hack squat leg press combo",
      "pendulum squat machine",
      "plate loaded hack squat",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Best Dedicated Hack Squat: Titan Fitness Plate-Loaded Linear Hack Squat Machine", link: PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/home-gym/">Home Gym</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Buying Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Best Hack Squat Machines for a Home Gym</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        A home hack squat can be a dedicated sled, one half of a leg press combo, a pendulum lever or a commercial-style hack press. Here are six on sale now, how the sled angle changes what the plates feel like, and the shoulder-pad, safety-stop, storage and floor checks to make before you buy.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated October 19, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
        </div>
        <a href="#top-picks" class="btn-ghost">View products</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We research every product on this page independently. If you buy through our links, we may earn a
        commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Learn more about how we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          The best hack squat machine for a home gym is the one whose angle, pads and stops fit
          your body and whose frame fits your room. A hack squat puts the load on your shoulders
          through pads, with your back against an inclined sled and your feet on a platform, so
          you can squat deep and hard with the path guided and no bar to balance. At home, most
          hack squats are sold as half of a leg press combo, but dedicated sleds, pendulum
          levers and hack presses are all on sale, and they feel very different. The six picks
          below cover those designs, and the guide after them explains angle, shoulder pads,
          safety stops, storage and space in plain terms.
        </p>

        <section id="top-picks" class="ed-toppicks not-prose">
          <h2>Our top picks:</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Dedicated Hack Squat:</span> <a href="#titan-linear-hack-squat">Titan Fitness Plate-Loaded Linear Hack Squat Machine</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Combo:</span> <a href="#titan-leg-press-hack-squat">Titan Fitness Leg Press Hack Squat Machine</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[1].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best for Small Spaces:</span> <a href="#force-usa-compact-hack-squat">Force USA Compact Leg Press & Hack Squat (2027 Model)</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[2].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Pads:</span> <a href="#body-solid-glph1100b-hack">Body-Solid GLPH1100B Leg Press & Hack Squat</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[3].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Pendulum:</span> <a href="#bells-of-steel-pandemonium">Bells of Steel Pandemonium Squat</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[4].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Most Commercial:</span> <a href="#steelflex-plhp">Steelflex PLHP Plate Loaded Hack Press</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[5].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the products below.</strong> We have not tested these
          machines, and no figure on this page is our own measurement. Each was confirmed as
          currently sold on 19 October 2026 on the maker's own store (Titan Fitness, Bells of Steel,
          Body-Solid and Force USA) or, for the Steelflex, at a US dealer. Angle, capacity,
          dimensions and price were checked on that date against at least one independent source
          where one existed — Garage Gym Reviews, Iron Company, indexed Amazon listings and a UK
          retailer. A figure appears in a product's specification line only where the maker and an
          independent source agree; where they conflicted, we left it out and say so on the card.
          Three machines (Titan's dedicated hack squat, the Bells of Steel Pandemonium and the
          Steelflex) have little or no independent coverage, so their figures are the maker's and
          are labelled as such. We chose machines sold on Amazon US where we could find them.
          Amazon's own pages would not load for us, so Amazon availability was confirmed from
          indexed listings for the Titan combo and the Body-Solid only, and each card says where we
          could not confirm it. The buttons are tagged Amazon searches, so check the model name and
          seller. Every brand was checked against the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall
          database. Prices are those we saw on that date and change often. We earn a commission if
          you buy through the Amazon links on this page; it does not change which products we list
          or what we say about them. <strong class="text-gray-900">What this page is, and is not.</strong> We review home
          exercise equipment; we are not giving medical, physiotherapy, coaching or structural
          engineering advice. Rated capacities are manufacturer figures, not a guide to what you
          should lift. A hack squat loads the knees, hips and spine through the shoulders and
          raises blood pressure under heavy effort; if you have a knee, hip, back, neck or heart
          condition, high blood pressure, are pregnant or postpartum, or are returning from injury
          or surgery, talk to a qualified professional before starting or changing a programme.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#quick-picks">Our Picks at a Glance</a></li>
            <li><a href="#picks">The 6 Best Hack Squat Machines for a Home Gym</a></li>
            <li><a href="#how-we-chose">How We Chose These Hack Squat Machines</a></li>
            <li><a href="#types">Dedicated, Combo, Pendulum and Hack Press</a></li>
            <li><a href="#sled-angle">Sled Angle and Why the Same Plates Feel Different</a></li>
            <li><a href="#shoulder-pads">Shoulder Pads, Back Pad and Fit</a></li>
            <li><a href="#safety-stops">Safety Stops, Lockouts and Training Alone</a></li>
            <li><a href="#space-storage">Footprint, Plate Storage and Floor Loading</a></li>
            <li><a href="#who-should-skip">Who Should Buy Something Else</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

