        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best hack squat machine for a home gym?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For a dedicated hack squat, Titan's Plate-Loaded Linear Hack Squat, a 35-degree sled on linear bearings for about $975, though its figures are Titan's own. For a machine that also leg presses, Titan's Leg Press Hack Squat combo, whose size, weight and angle three sources agree on. For a short room, the Force USA Compact at 30 degrees.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a hack squat or leg press better for a home gym?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It depends on what you want to train. A hack squat loads you through the shoulders and moves your body with the sled, so it feels closer to a squat and is harder with the same plates. A leg press keeps your body still and loads only through the feet. A combo does both in one footprint, which is why most home buyers choose one.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What angle should a hack squat machine be?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              There is no single right angle. Most combos run at about 45 degrees; Titan's dedicated sled is 35 and Force USA's Compact 30. A steeper sled puts more of your body weight and the plates into the lift; a shallower one leans you back and feels lighter. Compare numbers only on the same machine.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why does a hack squat feel heavier than a leg press?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On a hack squat your body rides the sled, so you lift a share of your own weight as well as the plates: about 71% of it on a 45-degree sled, ignoring friction. On a leg press your body stays still. Garage Gym Reviews also found the hack squat on Titan's combo heavier than on most commercial machines.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much space does a home hack squat machine need?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Titan lists its dedicated hack squat at 46.75 by 60.5 inches, the Force USA Compact is 52 by 65, and the leg press combos are 84 to 96 inches long. Allow about three feet around the frame to step in, load plates and swap pads. The Pandemonium is about 88 inches tall, so check the ceiling for that one.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a hack squat machine safe to use alone?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is one of the safer ways to squat hard alone, provided you set the bottom stop just below your deepest safe position every session, practise the lockout levers with an empty sled, load plates evenly and stay within the user weight limit. Talk to a qualified professional first if you have a knee, back, neck or heart condition or high blood pressure.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

